<td id="S2.T2.2.1.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bbbb ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T2.2.1.6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">15.4</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Vietnamese phoneme recognition results of architectural combination.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Phone-to-Text:</span>
Phone-to-Text (P2T) conversion is framed as a sequence-to-sequence (Seq2Seq) translation problem, drawing inspiration from Machine Translation (MT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. In this work, the phoneme sequence serves as the <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“source language”</span> and the text as the <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“target language”</span>. Seq2Seq architecture enables an inherent design for the P2T module to map a phoneme input sequence to a text sequence.
In this work, we choose T5 model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> for the P2T model, which frames all NLP problems as text2text generation tasks. As discussed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, the model demonstrates its effectiveness in handling phonetic transformations, successfully applying T5 to Grapheme-to-Phoneme (G2P) conversion. Similarly, we also leverage T5 model design for the inverse problem of phoneme-to-text translation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Two-stage TSPC model:</span>
The integration of two separate models in a unified architecture is not straightforward, as the incorrect phone sequences generated by S2P model can introduce noises in the input of P2T model, which reduces the performance of the translation task. To resolve this issue, the TSPC model carefully combines S2P and P2T modules and performs joint fine-tuning as shown in Fig <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.2 Two-stage Model Development ‣ 2 Phoneme-Centric Model ‣ TSPC: A Two-Stage Phoneme-Centric Architecture for code-switching Vietnamese-English Speech Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
To integrate the pre-trained S2P model with a T5 architecture in P2T for robust text generation, we freeze S2P parameters during the tuning phase to ensure consistent phonetic sequence production. The information from the S2P model can enrich the P2T Econder via a cross-attention, which serves as an intermediate bridge, encouraging the T5 Encoder to simultaneously capture linguistic and original acoustic features. In particular, the T5 Encoder’s cross-attention mechanism utilizes the final decoding hidden states from the S2P model as <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> matrices, while the output of the self-attention layer within the T5 Encoder serves as the <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> matrix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For the text generation component, inspired by recent advancements in large language models where pre-trained Decoders are effectively employed by freezing their parameters and integrating them with trainable modules to achieve strong performance in various downstream tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, we adopt the similar strategy. In this work, we specifically leverage the pre-trained T5 Decoder and freeze its parameters during the training phase, enabling the Decoder to fully exploit its extensive pre-trained linguistic knowledge, effectively translating the enriched features from the T5 Encoder into text.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T3" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S2.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:241.5pt;height:61.8pt;vertical-align:-29.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-70.9pt,18.1pt) scale(0.630153632963726,0.630153632963726) ;">
